Let the number of pennies Alex has be \( x \) and the number of pennies Bob has be \( y \). According to the problem, if Alex gives Bob a penny, Bob will have three times as many pennies as Alex has. This can be written as the equation: \( y + 1 = 3(x - 1) \). Simplifying this, we get: \( y + 1 = 3x - 3 \) or \( y = 3x - 4 \). The second condition states that if Bob gives Alex a penny, Bob will have twice as many pennies as Alex has. This can be written as the equation: \( y - 1 = 2(x + 1) \). Simplifying this, we get: \( y - 1 = 2x + 2 \) or \( y = 2x + 3 \). Now we have a system of linear equations: \[
\begin{cases}
y = 3x - 4 \\
y = 2x + 3
\end{cases}
\] Equating the two expressions for \( y \), we get: \( 3x - 4 = 2x + 3 \). Solving for \( x \), we get: \( x = 7 \). Substituting \( x = 7 \) back into the equation \( y = 3x - 4 \), we get: \( y = 3(7) - 4 = 21 - 4 = 17 \). Therefore, Bob currently has 17 pennies.</think> <answer>17</answer>

